00:30:21,940 --> 00:30:24,940
It would become known as the current that powered the first

509
00:30:24,941 --> 00:30:27,940
electric chair.

510
00:30:27,941 --> 00:30:31,940
The chair was not built by Westinghouse, but by Edison.

511
00:30:31,941 --> 00:30:36,380
Death by electrocution was new in 1888, and Edison actively

512
00:30:36,381 --> 00:30:39,940
campaigned for the selection of an AC chair, hoping to

513
00:30:39,941 --> 00:30:42,980
discourage future consumer AC systems.

514
00:30:42,981 --> 00:30:48,380
Now, the scheme was to make the first electrocution with

515
00:30:48,381 --> 00:30:53,860
alternating current, and then say, do you want the

516
00:30:53,861 --> 00:30:57,460
electrocutioner's current in your house?

517
00:30:57,461 --> 00:31:02,460
The Edison types located a Westinghouse generator

518
00:31:03,460 --> 00:31:07,360
and moved it into the New York State prison.

519
00:31:08,460 --> 00:31:13,460
And then a poor man named Kemler was scheduled for execution.

520
00:31:19,460 --> 00:31:24,460
The switch was pulled and he looked as if he were executed,

521
00:31:24,461 --> 00:31:30,700
electrocuted, if you will, but actually he wasn't.

522
00:31:30,701 --> 00:31:33,700
He still had some life in him.

523
00:31:36,700 --> 00:31:41,980
It was a rather bungled job. The press echoed the Edison

524
00:31:41,981 --> 00:31:45,340
organization's campaign that characterized AC power as an

525
00:31:45,341 --> 00:31:47,580
unwelcome entity in the American home.

526
00:31:47,581 --> 00:31:51,020
The public, however, for very practical reasons, continued to

527
00:31:51,021 --> 00:31:54,300
support the stronger AC current. It eventually became the

528
00:31:54,301 --> 00:31:59,300
across the country. Edison never accepted

529
00:31:59,301 --> 00:32:02,300
alternating current. I've never encountered a

530
00:32:02,301 --> 00:32:06,300
statement from him recognizing its superiority.

531
00:32:06,301 --> 00:32:10,300
On the other hand, the company he founded, after he withdrew

532
00:32:10,301 --> 00:32:14,300
from the management of the company, bought into alternating

533
00:32:14,301 --> 00:32:17,300
current. While Edison was losing

534
00:32:17,301 --> 00:32:21,300
the AC versus DC battle, he busied himself with the

535
00:32:21,301 --> 00:32:24,300
development of many other of the modern conveniences in the

536
00:32:24,301 --> 00:32:27,340
take for granted. One was the battery-powered

537
00:32:27,341 --> 00:32:30,300
electric motor. And this is an electric motor

538
00:32:30,301 --> 00:32:33,300
that was sold as a fan. You know, stop and think

539
00:32:33,301 --> 00:32:35,300
about it. That's something pretty remarkable,

540
00:32:35,301 --> 00:32:39,300
right? In the hot, humid weather in the East Coast, the Midwest,

541
00:32:39,301 --> 00:32:44,300
and the South, having a fan, you know, in a hot office or a home,

542
00:32:44,301 --> 00:32:48,300
that really begins to change your life. Most significantly,

543
00:32:48,301 --> 00:32:51,300
after a decade of neglect, Edison turned his attention back

544
00:32:51,301 --> 00:32:55,100
attention back to the invention he described as his baby, the

545
00:32:55,101 --> 00:32:58,700
phonograph. By the time Edison worked his way

546
00:32:58,701 --> 00:33:02,700
around to the phonograph again, his patents had lapsed, and

547
00:33:02,701 --> 00:33:04,700
others had developed more practical versions of the

548
00:33:04,701 --> 00:33:08,700
phonograph that replaced tin foil as a recording medium with

549
00:33:08,701 --> 00:33:12,700
wax cylinders. Spurred by his competitive streak,

550
00:33:12,701 --> 00:33:15,700
Edison formed the Edison Phonograph Corporation to

551
00:33:15,701 --> 00:33:18,700
market and sell updated versions of his phonograph.

552
00:33:18,701 --> 00:33:21,500
These turn-of-the-century models were leagues ahead

553
00:33:21,501 --> 00:33:24,700
of Edison's original experiments.

554
00:33:24,701 --> 00:33:27,700
To reach a much greater number of people,

555
00:33:27,701 --> 00:33:31,240
several companies would lease phonographs,

556
00:33:31,241 --> 00:33:35,240
and they would set them up in parlors or arcades,

557
00:33:35,241 --> 00:33:40,160
where they may have a row of five or six or 10 or 12

558
00:33:40,161 --> 00:33:42,360
penny-in-the-slot phonographs.

559
00:33:42,361 --> 00:33:44,700
The way this machine operates...

560
00:33:44,701 --> 00:33:49,800
all by battery.

561
00:33:49,801 --> 00:33:50,940
It's all electric.

562
00:33:50,941 --> 00:33:54,140
The penny would drop, activate the switch

563
00:33:54,141 --> 00:33:56,840
and the mechanism driven by the battery,

564
00:33:56,841 --> 00:34:03,460
start the record playing, and the listener would

565
00:34:03,461 --> 00:34:06,860
put on a set of earphones and listen to the record

566
00:34:06,861 --> 00:34:10,660
for the duration of two minutes.

567
00:34:10,661 --> 00:34:12,700
Edison, the businessman, saw another use

568
00:34:12,701 --> 00:34:15,940
use for his phonograph. Talking dolls may be common

569
00:34:15,941 --> 00:34:19,600
today, but the world was again taken aback when Edison introduced

570
00:34:19,601 --> 00:34:24,440
his prototype to the market. Well, this is our friend,

571
00:34:24,441 --> 00:34:28,940
the Edison doll. And this was interesting, of

572
00:34:28,941 --> 00:34:33,060
course, because inside of the doll was this little motor.

573
00:34:33,061 --> 00:34:36,560
And to play the motor, this crank stuck out behind the

574
00:34:36,561 --> 00:34:39,260
dolls, and you crank the motor like that.

575
00:34:39,261 --> 00:34:42,260
and this little horn, the body was made of tin,

576
00:34:42,261 --> 00:34:45,260
and it was punctured with little holes.

577
00:34:45,261 --> 00:34:48,260
And the records, of course, are even rarer than the dolls,

578
00:34:48,261 --> 00:34:50,260
because they're little wax records.

579
00:34:50,261 --> 00:34:52,260
They're terribly fragile.

580
00:34:52,261 --> 00:34:56,260
If a child dropped the doll, the record would almost invariably break.

581
00:34:56,261 --> 00:34:59,260
The talking doll was not a success.

582
00:34:59,261 --> 00:35:03,260
And after several years, Edison was advised to get out of that business.

583
00:35:03,261 --> 00:35:08,260
Rumor has it that all of the excess dolls were buried in a pit

584
00:35:08,261 --> 00:35:10,700
in West Orange at the Edison Laboratories.

585
00:35:11,600 --> 00:35:14,660
And there they've been for over 100 years.

586
00:35:17,560 --> 00:35:20,680
Thomas Edison's renewed involvement with recorded sound

587
00:35:20,681 --> 00:35:23,300
led him to begin pondering a new technology.

588
00:35:23,940 --> 00:35:25,500
I got Edison thinking, you know,

589
00:35:25,640 --> 00:35:29,600
maybe I can do for the eye what the phonograph does for the ear.

590
00:35:30,200 --> 00:35:33,280
At the age of 41, when most successful men

591
00:35:33,281 --> 00:35:35,000
were content to rest on their laurels,

592
00:35:35,001 --> 00:35:40,000
Edison was poised to ignite yet another revolution, the science

593
00:35:40,001 --> 00:35:42,000
and art of motion pictures.

594
00:35:47,000 --> 00:35:51,000
In 1892, Thomas Edison's company, Edison General Electric,

595
00:35:51,001 --> 00:35:55,000
merged with the Thompson Houston Company to form General

596
00:35:55,001 --> 00:35:58,000
Electric, which ironically made its fortune selling AC power

597
00:35:58,001 --> 00:35:59,000
equipment.

598
00:36:05,000 --> 00:36:07,000
The End

599
00:36:15,000 --> 00:36:17,080
If Thomas Edison were alive today,

600
00:36:17,081 --> 00:36:20,280
he'd no doubt be fascinated by a visit to the local IMAX

601
00:36:20,281 --> 00:36:22,120
movie theater.

602
00:36:22,121 --> 00:36:25,680
Here he'd find a screen the size of a five-story building,

603
00:36:25,681 --> 00:36:28,160
a projector larger than a refrigerator,

604
00:36:28,161 --> 00:36:30,600
and film reels bigger than a dinner table.

605
00:36:30,601 --> 00:36:33,560
Here you see a platter with a film of approximately 70

606
00:36:33,561 --> 00:36:36,120
minutes in length. Film platters can weigh as much

607
00:36:36,121 --> 00:36:41,740
as 500 to 600 pounds. The projection system uses a

608
00:36:41,741 --> 00:36:44,800
technology called rolling loop. It's been in all of our

609
00:36:44,801 --> 00:36:49,340
historical products. As impressive as state-of-the-art

610
00:36:49,341 --> 00:36:53,220
film technology is, its basic components were present more

611
00:36:53,221 --> 00:36:56,620
than a century ago, when Edison first premiered his

612
00:36:56,621 --> 00:37:02,060
kinetoscope. There was the electric lamp,

613
00:37:02,061 --> 00:37:05,060
version of rolling loop technology, which combined to

614
00:37:05,061 --> 00:37:08,060
create the illusion of moving pictures.

615
00:37:08,061 --> 00:37:12,060
The idea didn't spring out of thin air.

616
00:37:12,061 --> 00:37:16,060
In 1888, Edison became fascinated with the work of

617
00:37:16,061 --> 00:37:19,060
Edward Muybridge, a naturalist and photographer who developed

618
00:37:19,061 --> 00:37:22,060
a system of photographing the movement of running horses with

619
00:37:22,061 --> 00:37:26,060
a series of still cameras triggered with tripwires.

620
00:37:26,061 --> 00:37:31,360
The result was a series of sequential photographs.

621
00:37:31,460 --> 00:37:34,300
For the first time, there was a photographically accurate

622
00:37:34,400 --> 00:37:35,700
illusion of movement.

623
00:37:39,300 --> 00:37:42,680
Working with collaborator William Kennedy Laurie Dixon,

624
00:37:42,780 --> 00:37:46,740
Edison created his kinetoscope, a battery-powered peep show

625
00:37:46,840 --> 00:37:49,120
machine that would become a major draw in penny arcades

626
00:37:49,220 --> 00:37:50,420
all over America.

627
00:37:52,520 --> 00:37:54,620
This is an Edison kinetoscope.

628
00:37:54,621 --> 00:37:58,620
This is the way he commercialized motion pictures.

629
00:37:58,621 --> 00:38:02,620
And it plays 35mm film.

630
00:38:02,621 --> 00:38:06,620
Edison invented 35mm film, the same film we use today.

631
00:38:10,620 --> 00:38:14,620
The film runs across this pool.

632
00:38:14,621 --> 00:38:18,620
If you look down through here, you can see the picture.

633
00:38:20,620 --> 00:38:23,620
As each frame passes under the lens,

634
00:38:23,621 --> 00:38:26,060
the lens. The shutter permits a flash of

635
00:38:26,061 --> 00:38:30,220
light so brief the frame appears to be frozen.

636
00:38:30,221 --> 00:38:33,860
The eye takes in 24 of these still frames each second, which

637
00:38:33,861 --> 00:38:36,860
produces the illusion of movement in the brain.

638
00:38:41,180 --> 00:38:44,380
Edison's contribution to cinema also included the first motion

639
00:38:44,381 --> 00:38:48,180
picture studio. The first motion picture

640
00:38:48,181 --> 00:38:52,420
studio ever set up in the world was in the yard of my laboratory

641
00:38:52,421 --> 00:38:55,520
of West Orange. The building revolved to follow

642
00:38:55,521 --> 00:38:58,420
the sun. We dubbed it the Black Moriah

643
00:38:58,421 --> 00:39:03,660
because it was covered with black paper inside and out.

644
00:39:03,661 --> 00:39:06,660
Edison's inventive control of sunlight allowed him to shoot

645
00:39:06,661 --> 00:39:10,780
very clear images against the black paper backgrounds.

646
00:39:10,781 --> 00:39:14,780
The shooting stage was barely 12 feet square, but big enough to

647
00:39:14,781 --> 00:39:21,180
shoot everyday activities like the kiss and the sneeze.

648
00:39:21,181 --> 00:39:25,860
short, simple studies of movement that captivated the first viewers of cinema.

649
00:39:28,860 --> 00:39:31,960
Edison even filmed boxing matches inside the Black Mariah.

650
00:39:32,980 --> 00:39:35,160
Each round played on a different kinetoscope.

651
00:39:35,820 --> 00:39:38,860
The viewer needed a pocketful of nickels to see the entire match.

652
00:39:39,480 --> 00:39:42,460
Well, in a sense, I guess you could credit Edison with the first pay-per-view.

653
00:39:42,461 --> 00:39:45,840
You could see boxing matches for the first time on film.

654
00:39:45,940 --> 00:39:47,200
You didn't have to attend the match.

655
00:39:47,201 --> 00:39:50,700
But perhaps Edison's greatest legacy in film

656
00:39:50,800 --> 00:39:54,640
were scenes he documented outside the studio.

657
00:39:54,740 --> 00:39:57,440
In later life, he developed a passion for taking the camera

658
00:39:57,540 --> 00:40:00,520
new places to create a motion picture record of the world

659
00:40:00,620 --> 00:40:03,440
in a fixed time and place.

660
00:40:03,540 --> 00:40:06,960
Thomas Edison took the camera in a lot of very interesting

661
00:40:07,060 --> 00:40:08,060
places.

662
00:40:08,160 --> 00:40:11,360
He was in love with taking movies.

663
00:40:11,460 --> 00:40:16,000
He really thought that it was the way to preserve the present.

664
00:40:16,001 --> 00:40:21,000
And a lot of the newsreels that you can dig through were

665
00:40:21,100 --> 00:40:25,000
actually photographed by him or his staff.

666
00:40:25,100 --> 00:40:28,140
These documentary images interested Edison far more

667
00:40:28,240 --> 00:40:31,040
than the emerging commercial cinema, which was presenting

668
00:40:31,140 --> 00:40:34,060
more and more complex narrative stories projected in theaters

669
00:40:34,160 --> 00:40:35,460
on a screen.

670
00:40:35,560 --> 00:40:39,960
As the 20th century dawned, Edison

671
00:40:40,060 --> 00:40:42,600
continued to revolutionize the world with his innovative

672
00:40:42,700 --> 00:40:43,960
designs.

673
00:40:43,961 --> 00:40:49,960
Some were so far ahead of their time that we're just beginning to rediscover them today.

674
00:40:52,960 --> 00:40:55,960
Edison was looking for another project to work on,

675
00:40:55,961 --> 00:41:00,960
and automobiles were beginning to emerge as an exciting new technology.

676
00:41:00,961 --> 00:41:03,960
There were internal combustion engines, there were steam engines,

677
00:41:03,961 --> 00:41:06,960
and there were people who thought, hey, electricity.

678
00:41:06,961 --> 00:41:10,960
So they were building storage batteries for automobiles like this one,

679
00:41:10,961 --> 00:41:12,960
and Edison began working on this project.

680
00:41:13,960 --> 00:41:20,960
So this is what we have 100 years later in the vision of Thomas Edison.

681
00:41:20,961 --> 00:41:28,960
His concept of batteries for electric power, stationary applications are now being utilized to propel motor vehicles.

682
00:41:28,961 --> 00:41:35,960
In many ways it looks like an automobile today under the hood,

683
00:41:35,961 --> 00:41:39,960
but rather than a reciprocating engine, combustion engine here,

684
00:41:39,961 --> 00:41:42,860
This is a power electronics module that is used to control

685
00:41:42,960 --> 00:41:45,860
the vehicle. The batteries are

686
00:41:45,960 --> 00:41:48,560
underneath the vehicle. When the driver presses the

687
00:41:48,660 --> 00:41:52,000
accelerator, an electric current flows from the battery to a

688
00:41:52,100 --> 00:41:55,800
motorized drive shaft that propels the vehicle.

689
00:41:59,820 --> 00:42:03,520
At universities all across America, Edison is inspiring new

690
00:42:03,620 --> 00:42:06,360
generations of inventors to grapple with the problems of

691
00:42:06,361 --> 00:42:10,200
present with the simple materials around them.

692
00:42:10,300 --> 00:42:13,160
What is happening today is that we have, in addition

693
00:42:13,260 --> 00:42:16,900
to alternating current, coming from a major central power

694
00:42:17,000 --> 00:42:21,800
plant, the ability to generate power on the site.

695
00:42:21,900 --> 00:42:24,520
That's called distributed generation.

696
00:42:24,620 --> 00:42:28,720
And the type of generators that are evolving are generators that

697
00:42:28,820 --> 00:42:33,860
produce direct current, not alternating current.

698
00:42:33,960 --> 00:42:36,360
This building at the University of California

699
00:42:36,361 --> 00:42:39,600
California at Irvine has a robotic power manager called

700
00:42:39,601 --> 00:42:44,160
Next Tech that can choose solar power first when available, and

701
00:42:44,161 --> 00:42:46,800
AC power from the local power company when solar isn't

702
00:42:46,801 --> 00:42:50,640
available. It can store solar power and convert it to direct

703
00:42:50,641 --> 00:42:54,120
current for maximum efficiency in the operation of such DC

704
00:42:54,121 --> 00:42:57,320
devices as computers and lighting.

705
00:42:57,321 --> 00:43:00,020
Currently, power is lost when alternating current is

706
00:43:00,021 --> 00:43:04,260
transformed to direct current. Our fluorescent lights, for

707
00:43:04,261 --> 00:43:08,260
for example, are direct current devices, which are low

708
00:43:08,261 --> 00:43:11,460
efficiency today on alternating current.

709
00:43:11,461 --> 00:43:15,060
By using direct current, Edison's vision, we're able to

710
00:43:15,061 --> 00:43:17,660
operate them much more efficiently.

711
00:43:17,661 --> 00:43:20,860
So that's just one example of the way the direct current will

712
00:43:20,861 --> 00:43:24,860
in the future be used in buildings.

713
00:43:24,861 --> 00:43:27,860
When Thomas Alva Edison receives the highest honor his

714
00:43:27,861 --> 00:43:31,660
country can bestow, the Congressional Medal of Honor...

715
00:43:31,661 --> 00:43:35,060
In 1928, Thomas Edison was presented the Congressional Gold

716
00:43:35,061 --> 00:43:38,300
Medal for his contributions to the improvement of life in the

717
00:43:38,301 --> 00:43:41,300
United States. It's the highest honor an American

718
00:43:41,301 --> 00:43:44,300
civilian can receive. I firmly believe that the

719
00:43:44,301 --> 00:43:48,300
machine age will favorably affect the lives of the workers.

720
00:43:48,301 --> 00:43:52,420
It is impossible, however, to forecast what electricity and

721
00:43:52,421 --> 00:43:57,820
invention will make of the world a hundred years hence.

722
00:43:57,821 --> 00:44:03,120
Edison died in 1931 at the age of 84.

723
00:44:03,121 --> 00:44:05,820
The nation dimmed its light bulbs for one minute on the day

724
00:44:05,821 --> 00:44:09,120
of his funeral to commemorate this great American.

725
00:44:13,820 --> 00:44:18,960
In light, sound, and power, his legacy surrounds us today,

726
00:44:18,961 --> 00:44:21,820
and will continue to do so well into the future.

